.TH LOCALPASSKEY 1 "October 2026" "localpasskey 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
localpasskey \- list, restore, delete, export and import the passkeys and authenticator codes stored on this computer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B localpasskey list
.RB [ \-\-trash ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.br
.B localpasskey delete
.RI [ ID ...]
.RB [ \-\-site
.IR site ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B localpasskey restore
.RI [ ID ...]
.RB [ \-\-site
.IR site ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B localpasskey purge
.I ID
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.br
.B localpasskey pin
.BR status | set | remove
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.br
.B localpasskey totp list
.RB [ \-\-trash ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.br
.B localpasskey totp add
.RB [ \-\-site
.IR site ]
.RB [ \-\-issuer
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-account
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B localpasskey totp code
.I query
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.br
.B localpasskey totp edit
.I ID
.RB [ \-\-site
.IR site ]
.RB [ \-\-issuer
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-account
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.br
.B localpasskey totp
.BR delete | restore
.RI [ ID ...]
.RB [ \-\-site
.IR site ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B localpasskey totp purge
.I ID
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.br
.B localpasskey totp settings
.RB [ \-\-require\-password
.BR yes | no ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.br
.B localpasskey export
.I file
.RI [ ID ...]
.RB [ \-\-site
.IR site " | " \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-format
.BR localpasskey | cxf ]
.RB [ \-\-unencrypted ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-codes ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.br
.B localpasskey import
.I file
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR user ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B localpasskey status
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B localpasskey
talks to
.BR localpasskeyd (8),
which keeps passkeys for websites on this computer. Every user only sees
and manages their own passkeys; root can act on any user's with
.BR \-\-user .
.PP
.B delete
does not destroy a passkey: it moves it to the trash, where it stops
working but can still be listed and brought back. Passkeys stay in the
trash until
.B purge
removes one for good; nothing expires on its own.
Each user can keep 4096 passkeys; passkeys in the trash count until purged.
Every command that changes something asks for your password through polkit
(except turning the authenticator code password back on, see
.BR "totp settings" ),
and over SSH uses
.BR pkttyagent (1)
to ask in the terminal.
.PP
A PIN protects the passkeys against a browser listing or deleting them.
Browsers ask for it in their security-key settings, and
.B localpasskey pin
sets, shows or removes it.
.PP
.B localpasskey totp
manages authenticator codes: the time-based one-time codes (TOTP, RFC 6238)
of authenticator apps, for sites that ask for one as a second step. Their
setup keys are kept by the service beside your passkeys, with the same
protection, and leave it only in an export you approve. Codes complement
passkeys and do not replace them: a fake site can ask for a code and pass it
on, while a passkey only ever signs in to the site it belongs to.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
Show your passkeys: an ID, the site, the account, whether it is a
.I passkey
(usable without typing a user name) or a
.I security key
(a second factor the site asks for by ID), and when it was created and last
used.
.B \-\-json
prints all details.
.B \-\-trash
shows the trash instead, with the time each passkey was moved there in the
last column.
.TP
.B delete
Move the passkeys given by
.I ID
(or any unique prefix of at least four characters), all passkeys for
.BR \-\-site ,
or
.B \-\-all
of them to the trash. The command shows what it will move and asks for
confirmation (skip with
.BR \-y ),
then polkit asks for your password. A passkey in the trash can no longer
sign you in anywhere, on any browser; bring it back with
.BR restore .
Deleting a passkey here does not remove it from the website's account
settings.
.TP
.B restore
Bring passkeys back from the trash, with the same
.IR ID ,
.B \-\-site
and
.B \-\-all
selectors as
.BR delete .
It refuses to restore a passkey while a newer passkey for the same account
on the same site exists, since the site would then have two live passkey IDs
for one account; delete the newer passkey first.
.TP
.B purge
Delete one passkey from the trash permanently. Give exactly one
.IR ID ,
and no
.BR \-y :
after showing the passkey, the command asks you to type the site name before
it asks for your password. This cannot be undone, and there is no way to
purge several passkeys at once.
.TP
.BR pin " " status | set | remove
Show, set or remove the PIN that browsers ask for in their security-key
settings before listing or deleting passkeys.
.B status
prints whether a PIN is set and how many attempts are left;
.B set
reads the new PIN twice without echoing it;
.B remove
forgets it. Only a hash of the PIN is stored, and the PIN on its own never
deletes a passkey: passkey changes always ask for your password.
.TP
.B totp list
Show your authenticator codes, but not the codes themselves: an ID, the
site, the issuer, the account, the type, and when the code was added. The
type is the number of digits and the period, with the algorithm when it is
not SHA1; an HOTP code shows its next counter instead of a period, and a
Steam code shows
.BR Steam .
.B \-\-json
prints all details.
.B \-\-trash
shows the trash instead, with the time each code was moved there.
.TP
.B totp add
Save an authenticator code. Paste the
.B otpauth://totp/
link or the setup key (Base32; spaces, dashes and lower case are fine) that
the site shows next to its QR code. The command reads it from the terminal
without echoing it, or as one line from standard input, never from the
command line. It fills in the issuer and account from a link;
.B \-\-issuer
and
.B \-\-account
replace them. Without
.BR \-\-site ,
it asks for the website when standard input is a terminal, suggesting one
when the issuer looks like a host name, and otherwise takes that suggestion,
if any. A website with a scheme, path, port or user name, typed or given with
.BR \-\-site ,
is saved as its host name:
.B https://github.com/login
and
.B github.com:443
become
.BR github.com .
The command shows what it will save but never the setup key, and asks for
confirmation (skip with
.BR \-y )
before polkit asks for your password. When the setup key comes from standard
input, the confirmation is read from the terminal; when there is none, the
command stops before saving anything unless
.B \-y
is given.
.br
.B otpauth://hotp/
links are saved as counter-based codes, starting at their
.B counter
parameter.
.RB ( steam://
links and links with
.B \%encoder=steam )
are saved as Steam codes; LocalPasskey has no way to fetch a Steam secret, so
it must come from another app's Steam Guard setup. A link with any other
.B encoder
is refused.
.br
An
.B otpauth\-migration
link (Google Authenticator's *Transfer accounts* → *Export accounts*) holds
several codes: the command shows the plan, asks once, and lists the codes it
cannot read, such as MD5-based ones, with the reason. The website is what
.B \-\-site
selectors match, after the same reduction to a host name; it may be left
empty.
.TP
.BI "totp code " query
Print the current code of one authenticator code on standard output, and how
many more seconds it is valid on standard error. An HOTP code has no time
window: standard error shows the counter it used instead, and asking for the
code moves it on to its next counter, as the site does. Showing every code
at once never does.
.I query
is an ID prefix of at least four characters or part of the site, issuer or
account (case does not matter); if it matches several codes, the command
lists them and stops. When showing codes needs your password, polkit asks
for it each time.
.TP
.BI "totp edit " ID
Change the
.BR \-\-site ,
.B \-\-issuer
or
.B \-\-account
of a code. The setup key and the code type cannot be changed; add the code
again instead.
.TP
.BR "totp delete" ", " "totp restore" ", " "totp purge"
Move codes to the trash, bring them back, or delete one from the trash for
good, with the same selectors and confirmations as
.BR delete ,
.B restore
and
.BR purge .
The code of an entry in the trash is never shown. Restoring has no conflict rules.
.B purge
asks you to type the site, the issuer when the site is empty, or the word
.B delete
when both are, and always asks for your password. Without the code you may lose access to the account,
so turn off two-step verification on the site, or move it elsewhere, first.
Each user can keep 4096 authenticator codes besides the 4096 passkeys; codes
in the trash count until purged.
.TP
.B totp settings
Show whether your password is needed before codes are shown (yes by
default).
.B \-\-require\-password no
turns that off, which polkit asks your password for;
.B \-\-require\-password yes
turns it back on without asking.
.IP
While the setting is on, a program such as
.BR localpasskey-gui (1)
unlocks the codes once and may then show them until it has asked for no code
for 5 minutes, locks them, or exits, or until the service restarts. While a
program has them unlocked, adding, editing, deleting and restoring codes
does not ask again; permanent deletion always does. Each
.B totp code
command asks again. Root can read any user's codes with
.B \-\-user
without a dialog.
.TP
.B export
Write passkeys to
.IR file :
all live passkeys by default, or those given by
.IR ID ,
.B \-\-site
or
.BR \-\-all ,
as with
.BR delete .
Passkeys in the trash are never exported. The command shows what it will
export, asks twice for a new passphrase that encrypts the file (at least 8
characters, not echoed; it is not your login password), and then polkit asks
for your login password to allow the export. The file is a LocalPasskey backup:
the private keys, encrypted with AES-256-GCM under a key derived from the
passphrase with scrypt. Whoever has the file and the passphrase can sign in
as you; a forgotten passphrase cannot be recovered. The file is created with
mode 0600, and an existing file is only replaced with
.BR \-\-force .
.IP
.B \-\-format cxf
writes the FIDO Alliance Credential Exchange Format instead: a portable copy
that LocalPasskey and other tools that read CXF JSON files can import (most
password managers do not import such files directly). CXF is not encrypted, so it also needs
.BR \-\-unencrypted ,
and the command warns: anyone who can read the file has the passkeys.
CXF cannot say that a passkey is a security key (non-discoverable) or carry
its credProtect level; such passkeys come back as ordinary passkeys.
.IP
A LocalPasskey backup also holds your authenticator codes, setup keys
included, unless
.B \-\-no\-codes
is given: all live codes by default or with
.BR \-\-all ,
the codes saved for that website with
.BR \-\-site ,
and none when passkeys are chosen by
.IR ID .
The command lists them with the passkeys, and one password dialog allows
both. If the service cannot read your codes, the export stops before your
password is asked for and suggests
.BR \-\-no\-codes ,
which exports the passkeys alone. A backup with codes is format version 2, which LocalPasskey 0.3.1 and
earlier refuse to open rather than drop the codes; one that also holds an
HOTP or Steam code is version 3, for the same reason. A backup of passkeys,
or of passkeys and plain time-based codes, is unchanged. CXF files never hold codes, and with
.B \-\-format cxf
the command says so on standard error when you have any.
.TP
.B import
Add the passkeys in
.IR file ,
which may be a LocalPasskey backup (the command asks for its passphrase, and
again if it was wrong), a CXF file, or the unencrypted JSON export of
Bitwarden. Password-protected and account-encrypted Bitwarden exports are
refused; export again as unencrypted JSON. The command lists what it will
import and what it skips, with the reason, and asks for confirmation (skip
with
.BR \-y )
before polkit asks for your password. A passkey is skipped when it is
already on this computer (also in the trash), when a passkey for the same
account on the same site exists, when it appears twice in the file, when it
cannot be read, or when the store is full. The file is read and decrypted by
this command as you; the service only receives the passkeys, checks them,
and seals them for your user on this computer.
.IP
Authenticator codes in a LocalPasskey backup are imported too and listed in
a table of their own. A code is skipped when it is already on this computer
(the same code, or the same setup key for the same site and account), when
it is in the trash here, when it appears twice in the file, when it cannot
be read, or when the store is full.
.IP
LocalPasskey always reports a signature counter of 0. A site that has seen a
non-zero counter for an imported passkey may refuse it.
.TP
.B status
Check that the service is running, and show the virtual device node and how
many passkeys and authenticator codes are stored for you. If the codes cannot
be counted, the command says why on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ localpasskey list
ID            SITE        ACCOUNT                    TYPE     CREATED           LAST USED
3f9a1c2d55e0  github.com  octocat                    passkey  2026-10-08 06:31  2026-10-08 06:40
$ localpasskey delete 3f9a
Move this passkey to the trash? [y/N] y
Moved 1 passkey to the trash. Restore with `localpasskey restore`.
$ localpasskey restore 3f9a
Restore this passkey? [y/N] y
Restored 1 passkey.
$ localpasskey purge 3f9a
Type "github.com" to permanently delete this passkey. This cannot be undone.
> github.com
Permanently deleted the passkey for github.com.
$ localpasskey export passkeys.lpkbackup
New passphrase to encrypt the export (not your login password):
Confirm the passphrase:
Exported 3 passkeys to passkeys.lpkbackup.
$ localpasskey import passkeys.lpkbackup
Passphrase this file was exported with:
Import these 2 passkeys? [y/N] y
Imported 2 passkeys.
$ localpasskey totp add \-\-site github.com
otpauth:// link or setup key:
Save this authenticator code? [y/N] y
Saved the authenticator code for github.com.
$ localpasskey totp list
ID        SITE        ISSUER  ACCOUNT  TYPE             CREATED
9c4e07d2  github.com  GitHub  octocat  6 digits \[u00B7] 30 s  2026-10-09 18:02
$ localpasskey totp code github
492039
valid for 17 more seconds
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR localpasskeyd (8),
.BR localpasskey-bridge (1),
.BR localpasskey-gui (1)
